# Committed on purpose, and it holds no secrets — one path anchor, and the fact that Compose
# only reads it when THIS directory is the project directory is the whole point.
#
# Compose reads `.env` from the project directory, which is the directory of the first `-f`
# compose file (or the working directory when there is none). Run the fork on its own, or list
# `fork/docker-compose.yml` first, and this file is read: `.` is then this directory and every
# path in the compose file resolves here. List somebody else's compose file first and this file
# is NOT read, VORQ_FORK_DIR stays unset, and `docker compose` refuses the whole configuration
# rather than quietly mounting whatever happens to sit one level above their file.
#
# To compose this fork after another stack, export VORQ_FORK_DIR as an absolute path:
#   VORQ_FORK_DIR=/abs/path/to/vorq-evm-contracts/fork \
#     docker compose -f their.yml -f /abs/path/to/vorq-evm-contracts/fork/docker-compose.yml up
VORQ_FORK_DIR=.
